From cdfdd447bdf25f5433a3d3142052e976ab1585bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Blatter <noel.blatter@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 03:29:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Carrier system through the native kernel, SuperLU is loaded
 on first use

With NATIVE_LU the carrier system, a single sparse matrix, is factorized by the native
kernel like the signal systems (_SingleLU around _PatternLU). A run on x86-64 then never
loads SciPy's SuperLU module. Without the kernel, or when the kernel declines a matrix,
SuperLU does the work as before; it is loaded at the first factorization instead of at
the import of differometor.fast.

Measured in the emulated x86-64 image with one CPU, eight fresh runs in turn: grid
benchmark first run 470.2 -> 464.5 ms (forward 38.9 -> 34.6, imports 331.6 -> 330.4),
aLIGO benchmark 462.8 -> 455.1 ms. Loading the SuperLU module alone takes 0.7 ms on
arm64 Linux and 1.8 ms emulated. Without the kernel (arm64, macOS) the first run is
unchanged.

The carrier solution of the kernel is as close to the exact one as SuperLU's (residual
1e-16 on 18 setups, difference to a dense solve within the condition number). Outputs
of the grid benchmark move by 5e-13 (carrier), 1e-9 (signal and noise, as between the
kernel and SuperLU before), 4e-12 (sensitivity) and 3e-11 (gradient); the error against
the reference stays 5.3743e-06, for aLIGO 2.9e-11.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 differometor/__pycache__/fast.cpython-312.pyc | Bin 122989 -> 124726 bytes
 differometor/fast.py                          |  45 +++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 38 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

